136 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186483729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484136 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186484038 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484136 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186484057 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484136 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484136 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484136 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186484057 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484136 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "750f6f9e-b6e0-5035-8e03-025f023dadec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484220 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186484136 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484220 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37146" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484220 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484220 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}$ on the closed region  $D=\\{(x,y):{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9\\}.$ You are given that $f$ has a critical point at  $(1,0)$ and on the boundary of the region the points that could give rise to extreme values are  $(-3,0)$, and $(3,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484220 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "50860c27-301d-5231-9268-fbee285d7c09" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484220 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$ \n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](images/d7/d7864a36585a145e009846b23142372caeff3a68f17c9d1f7d1cf17e642f9543.png)\n\nSince the critical point $(1,0)$ lies inside $D,$ we include that point in the list of candidates for the extrema. \n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values.\n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(3,0),\\,(-3,0).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0) & =(1-1)^{2}+(0)^{2} \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(3,0) & =(2)^{2}+(0)^{2} \\\\  & =4 \\\\ f(-3,0) & =(-4)^{2}+(0)^{2} \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $0$ at the point $(1,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $16$ at the point $(-3,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484239 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186484125 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484239 #edb/ref 1069 "q-83400" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484239 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484239 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the global maximum of the function $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4x-2y$ on the closed triangular region  $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2-x\\}.$ You're given that $f$ has a critical point at $(1,1)$ and on the boundary of the triangle the points that could give rise to extreme values are the vertices." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484239 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "798ecbfd-5ca4-5e85-b538-375fc7b94df7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484239 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](images/30/30a739bfccac0a1ccc405b7ce1fd34afd46b5a2f436c852cef0bb38a13ec1e64.png)\n\n \n\nSince the point $(1,1)$ does not belong to the interior of $D$, we do not yet include it in the list of candidates for extrema (it lies on the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider it in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(0,2),\\,(2,0),\\,(0,0),\\,(1,1).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,2) & =2(0)^{2}+(2)^{2}-4(0)-2(2) \\\\  & =4-4 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(2,0) & =2(2)^{2}+(0)^{2}-4(2)-2(0) \\\\  & =8-8 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(0,0) & =2(0)^{2}+(0)^{2}-4(0)-2(0) \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(1,1) & =2(1)^{2}+(1)^{2}-4(1)-2(1) \\\\  & =2+1-4-2 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-3$ at the point $(1,1),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $0$ at the points $(0,0),(0,2),$ and $(2,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484299 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186484346 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484299 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0072a223-d977-5b15-a887-a851fc97c6d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484299 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186404350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484306 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186484315 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484306 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "13d0691c-2114-546b-9bbe-2348cd351497" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484306 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186484220 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484315 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186484340 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484315 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "37a5ea02-37b7-5758-8571-8626bf94da23" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484315 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484340 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186484299 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484340 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "af6a13b7-97bf-5a56-b47d-072d6ba6485e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484340 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186484239 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484346 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bcac2916-b3e2-509d-93c5-20d8fd6bdd8a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484